From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 22:12:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: lg-sw43402: record why the msm coupling cannot go
 upstream as-is

Verified on hardware: the flash is gone and the unblank is faster than the
80 ms timer it replaced, because display-on now lands one frame after enable
instead of waiting out a worst-case delay.

The shape is still not upstreamable, and that should be written down at the
place it would bite rather than discovered by whoever sends the patch. A panel
driver reaching into one specific DSI host is a layering violation DRM review
rejects; it only works because both live in this tree.

The blocker is that DRM has no hook: drm_panel_funcs offers prepare, enable,
disable and unprepare, and nothing that fires after the first commit. The
mainline sibling panel-lg-sw43408.c -- same LG family, also cmd-mode DSC --
has the same latent bug and papers over it with set_display_on followed by
msleep(50). That is exactly what this driver used to do, and what the flash
was.

So upstreaming means proposing the hook, not finding one. Noted as
TODO(upstream) with the argument already assembled: the general case, the
sibling as evidence it is not taimen-specific, and the vendor's own
mdss_mdp_cmd_kickoff() comment with its zero-wait post-panel-on command.

Kept in this form on purpose until someone builds that series. It is measured
and it works; the abstraction is worth doing once, properly, rather than
guessed at here.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
index 45984e284369..14188dce276f 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
@@ -25,6 +25,36 @@
  * and the msm DSI host is the only thing that counts completed cmd-mode
  * frames. See display_on_timeout_ms. Both are EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL there; if
  * either signature moves, this breaks at link time rather than silently.
+ *
+ * TODO(upstream): this shape is NOT upstreamable and must not be sent as-is.
+ * A panel driver reaching into one specific DSI host driver is a layering
+ * violation DRM review rejects, and it only works because both live in this
+ * tree.
+ *
+ * The blocker is that DRM has no hook for it: drm_panel_funcs offers
+ * prepare/enable/disable/unprepare and nothing that fires after the first
+ * frame is committed. Checked against this tree, 2026-09-20 -- and the
+ * mainline sibling panel-lg-sw43408.c, same LG family and also cmd-mode DSC,
+ * has the same latent bug and papers over it with
+ *
+ *     mipi_dsi_dcs_set_display_on_multi(&ctx);
+ *     mipi_dsi_msleep(&ctx, 50);
+ *
+ * i.e. a magic sleep, which is what this driver used to do and what the flash
+ * on every unblank actually was.
+ *
+ * So upstreaming means PROPOSING the hook, not finding one: a drm_panel_funcs
+ * or drm_bridge_funcs op invoked after the first commit, msm calling it, this
+ * panel using it. The general case is easy to argue -- a DSC cmd-mode panel
+ * cannot legally send display-on before GRAM has been written, every such
+ * panel hits it, and today they all guess a duration. The vendor's own
+ * mdss_mdp_cmd_kickoff() comment is the supporting evidence: "the panel is
+ * turned on only when we send the first frame ... to ensure that no artifacts
+ * are seen on the panel", with a zero wait field on its post-panel-on command.
+ *
+ * Kept in this form deliberately until then: it is measured, it works, and the
+ * abstraction that would make it upstreamable is worth building once, as a
+ * series, rather than guessed at here.
  */
 bool msm_dsi_wait_one_frame(u32 since, unsigned int timeout_ms);
 u32 msm_dsi_frames_done(void);
